**THE WIENER HORIZONTAL PASS RUNS C'S LANE WIDTH NOW — 16 i32 LANES -> 32 i16 —
AND THAT IS A 1.08-1.10x KERNEL WIN WITH DISJOINT SETS, WITH A FRAME-LEVEL WALL-CLOCK NULL, WHICH
IS REPORTED AS ONE.** The predecessor commit
(`wiener_avx512_tier_2026-09-05`) had to run 16 i32 lanes because published
magetypes had no path from a `u8` load to `i16x32` at 512 bits. archmage PR #97
built that path; this commit spends it. The kernel drops **30.6 % of its
instructions** (the horizontal fill alone **-46.5 %**) and **3.25-3.31 us ->
2.83-3.01 us** on the encoder's 64x64 proc unit, two disjoint sets over three
passes each on a box checked quiet. The frame wall clock does not move, in two independent passes each
against its own same-binary control — and it should not: the kernel is 0.68 %
of a preset-6 frame, so even a perfect 2x on it is below this harness's floor.

date:   2026-09-05
hosts:  MEASUREMENT r7900x (x86_64-linux, AMD Ryzen 9 7900X, **ZEN 4**, 24
        threads). GATES both ISAs (aarch64 = this Mac, M4 Pro).
base:   `origin/main e032898c`.
cand:   this commit.
C:      reference/svt-av1 @ 3115c0c1, the PREBUILT oracle, NOT rebuilt.
data:   benchmarks/wiener_h32_lanes_2026-09-05.tsv.

=================== 1. WHAT THE PR MADE POSSIBLE, EXACTLY ===================

The predecessor's doc comment named the gap precisely, and it was right:
`u8x64::widen_low()` reached `u16x32` and `u16x32` had **no**
`bitcast_i16x32`, while `U8x32Widen` was **not implemented for `X64V4Token`** —
so neither the 512-bit nor the 256-bit route from a byte load to signed 16-bit
lanes existed, and the kernel stayed in `i32x16`.

archmage **PR #97** (`feat/w512-signed-bitcasts`, head
`2524b0be162ca04ceb9a39906e19fe9c9255657e`, stacked on **#96**
`feat/integer-pairwise-absdiff`) generates the six `i16x32 <-> u16x32` value /
shared-ref / mutable-ref methods. Read from the fetched branch source, not from
the PR description: the generic impl is bounded on
`I16x32Backend + U16x32Backend` and goes through `simd_storage::cast`, so it
needs **no new backend trait** and works on every tier that has both — all five.
`U8x64Widen` is implemented for `X64V4Token`, `X64V4xToken`, `X64V3Token`,
`NeonToken`, `Wasm128Token` and `ScalarToken`. The chain
`u8x64::from_slice(..).widen_low().bitcast_i16x32()` therefore compiles and runs
on the whole ladder.

**One entry, not two.** #97 is stacked on #96, so the single rev carries both,
and `variance.rs`'s `abs_diff` / `madd_adjacent` calls (which #96 provides) keep
compiling against it — verified by building the workspace, not assumed.

=================== 2. WHAT DID *NOT* BECOME POSSIBLE ===================

`madd_adjacent` now exists, and C's vertical pass is built on it
(`convolve16_4tap_avx512`, `convolve_avx512.h:334`). **This commit does not use
it, and the reason is a second missing primitive.** `madd_adjacent` pairs
ADJACENT lanes; C reaches that layout with `_mm512_unpacklo_epi16` /
`unpackhi`, interleaving two rows. Checked in the #97 branch source rather than
assumed: `i16x32_impl.rs` exports `madd_adjacent` and `msub_adjacent` and **no**
`interleave`, `zip` or `unpack` of any name. So the vertical fold still widens
to `i32x16` and multiplies, behind the symmetry precondition, and **an
interleave / unpack primitive is the next thing that would close it.** That is
the honest successor to the #89 ask, and it is stated here rather than filed as
a new issue while #96/#97 are still open.

Also still not reproduced: C's `calc_zero_coef` 3/5/7-tap specialisation, and
its `maddubs`-on-byte-shuffled-data horizontal form (which needs a 64-byte
`shuffle_epi8`, also not exported).

=================== 3. WHAT LANDED ===================

`crates/svtav1-dsp/src/restoration.rs`, one body, five tiers, unchanged in
shape:

  * The intermediate ring and the widened source scratch become **i16**
    (`WIENER_SIMD_RING_W` 64 lanes of i16; `WIENER_SIMD_SRC_W` 96 -> 128,
    because the widening is now two `u8x64` loads and 128 is what two cover).
  * The row's bytes are copied into a zero-padded `[u8; 128]` and widened with
    `u8x64 -> widen_low/high -> u16x32 -> bitcast_i16x32`. The copy is not
    decoration: the load is 64 bytes wide and the block is only `w + 7`, so a
    direct load off the plane could read past its end at the right frame edge.
    The buffer is zeroed ONCE — `n_src` is constant across rows, so no stale
    sample can ever reach a lane.
  * The horizontal filter accumulates in `i16x32`: eight `mullo` and eight adds
    per 32 outputs, where it was eight `pmulld` per 16.
  * The vertical pass is unchanged except that its loads are now one `i16x32`
    per 32 columns instead of two `i32x16`.

**C's `wiener_clip_avx512` (wiener_convolve_avx512.c:27) is now reproduced, and
it is what makes i16 possible at all.** It shifts the tap sum BEFORE adding the
centre sample: `((S s*f + 4) >> 3) + (s[x+3] << 4) + 2048`. The scalar row
computes `((s[x+3] << 7) + 16384 + S s*f + 4) >> 3`. These are the same integer
for every sign, because `s[x+3] << 7` and `16384` are both multiples of 8 — an
exact regrouping, not an approximation. Only the first order keeps every
intermediate inside i16.

**The i16 rail is CHECKED, not assumed.** `wiener_simd_applicable` gains both
ends: `255 * Sf+ + 4 <= i16::MAX` and `255 * Sf- <= 32768`. Every prefix of the
accumulation lies in the same interval as the total, so bounding the two ends
bounds the whole reduction. For every filter the AV1 tap search can emit
(restoration.h:141-147) `Sf+ = Sf- <= 128`, i.e. `<= 32,640` against 32,767 —
but the function is public and takes an arbitrary `&[i16; 8]`, so a filter that
would wrap takes the scalar path instead of producing wrong pixels.

=================== 4. THE NUMBERS ===================

**THE KERNEL, which is the instrument that matches this change's size.** A new
`wiener_convolve_add_src_{64x64,64x8}` case in
`crates/svtav1-dsp/benches/kernel_tiers.rs`. zenbench, `min` per pass, three
passes per binary, quiet box (load 0.11-0.54, top process 3.2 % plymouthd). The
bench's x86 tier token is `X64V3Token`, so this prices the **AVX2** arm:

    64x64   base  3.31, 3.28, 3.25 us      -> set [3.25, 3.31]
            cand  2.98, 2.83, 3.01 us      -> set [2.83, 3.01]
            **THE SETS ARE DISJOINT.** median/median **1.101x**; the pairing
            that most favours the base (3.25 / 3.01) is still **1.080x**.
    64x8    base  548.9, 561.5 ns (two passes; the third and all three
            candidate passes were cut short when the session ended) — the
            small shape is INCOMPLETE and is reported as such. The
            discarded first sweep read base 554.6/546.6/562.2 ns and cand
            447.2/501.6/485.5 ns, i.e. it agreed with 64x64's direction, but
            it is not clean data and is not a result.

**THE KERNEL BY INSTRUCTION COUNT** (callgrind, and therefore the `_v3` arm —
valgrind masks AVX-512, verified again here: the profile names
`wiener_convolve_simd_v3` and never `_v4`):

    wiener kernel total        13,906,583 -> 9,657,143   (**-30.56 %**)
      the horizontal fill       5,377,216 -> 2,879,448   (**-46.45 %**)
      the rest of the arm       8,476,432 -> 6,712,160   (-20.81 %)
      the dispatch shell           52,935 ->    65,535   (+23.80 %)
    vs C's `svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_avx2` at 1,096,488:
      **12.68x C -> 8.81x C**

The horizontal fill's -46.45 % is the lane doubling landing almost exactly.

**THE FRAME — A NULL, AND SAID SO.** Frame Ir moves -0.306 % (photo_cid p6) and
-0.539 % (screen_terminal p6), which is what a 30 % cut to 0.68 % of a frame
looks like. `perf stat` (paired warmup-delta, median of 5, `taskset -c 20`):
photo instructions -0.156 % against a same-binary control that moved +0.030 %,
cycles +0.124 % against a control that moved -0.138 % — **inside the control**.
Wall clock, 21 interleaved paired rounds, every cell ident=Y, each A/B against
its own same-binary control in the same session:

    pass        cell                  CONTROL (p25-p75)        A/B (p25-p75)
    contended   photo_cid 512 p6   1.0023 (0.9992-1.0049)  1.0055 (1.0005-1.0074)
    contended   screen    512 p6   1.0073 (0.9811-1.0247)  1.0007 (0.9899-1.0112)
    quiet       photo_cid 512 p6   0.9999 (0.9933-1.0046)  1.0021 (0.9982-1.0058)
    quiet       screen    512 p6   1.0032 (0.9962-1.0101)  0.9996 (0.9881-1.0044)

**Every span overlaps its control, in both passes, and photo_cid leans
marginally NEGATIVE in both.** No frame-level wall-clock claim is made. This is
the same shape as `cdef_neon_4wide_i16_2026-09-05` — a real kernel win that the
frame cannot resolve — and it is reported the same way.

=================== 5. THE SILICON, AGAIN ===================

Everything above is **Zen 4**, where AVX-512 is double-pumped over 256-bit
units. The kernel bench prices the **AVX2** arm specifically (zenbench's x86
tier token is `X64V3Token`), and the `_v4` arm's evidence is the objdump census
(134 `zmm`, up from 108; the horizontal fill 219 -> 143 instructions at 99 ->
70 `zmm`) plus the byte gates, which on x86 all run natively with `_v4` live.
**Zen 5 (full-width 512-bit) was NOT measured** — the Zen 5 box on this network
is withdrawn, and the alternative quiet box is another Zen 4 part. A 512-bit
arm that gains little on a double-pumped part can gain materially on a
full-width one; do not extrapolate in either direction.

=================== 5b. A TIMED RUN THAT WAS PERTURBED, AND RE-TAKEN ===================

**The first kernel bench sweep is DISCARDED, not reported as a result.** It ran
21:08-21:35 UTC and a sibling lane was running valgrind on the same box for part
of that window — a ~50x single-threaded load. zenbench's `min` statistic is
fairly robust to that and the two sets came out disjoint anyway, but "fairly
robust" is not a measurement, and this campaign's rule is that a timed cell
taken under unknown contention is re-taken rather than argued about. Every
kernel number in section 4 is from the RE-RUN, on a box checked quiet
immediately before (top process 3.2 % plymouthd) with the lane's marker
claiming the window.

The first sweep's numbers, for the record and NOT used: 64x64 base 3.28 / 3.22 /
3.19 us, cand 2.85 / 2.86 / 2.91 us; 64x8 base 554.6 / 546.6 / 562.2 ns, cand
447.2 / 501.6 / 485.5 ns. That they agree with the re-run is reassurance, not
evidence.

**Two process fixes came out of it.** The lane marker now says *what kind* of
run is in flight ("TIMED ... do not run timed work"), which is what let the
contention be caught at all; and the marker is refreshed before each long run
rather than after. And zenbench was writing its result files to `/tmp/zenbench`
— `/tmp` is banned in this workspace precisely because a wipe once destroyed a
parity harness AND its pre-fix baseline. The re-run sets `TMPDIR` under `~/tmp`
and the earlier files were copied out before they could be lost.

=================== 6. THE DEPENDENCY PIN, AND ITS RISK ===================

**This commit adds NO new patch block.** The `percall`/`sse` lane landed one at
`d03f97357` pinning archmage/archmage-macros/magetypes to #96's `cc24398c`;
this commit BUMPS that single block's rev to #97's
`2524b0be162ca04ceb9a39906e19fe9c9255657e`. Because #97 is stacked on #96, the
one rev carries both and there is still exactly one block — a second, competing
block would have been the wrong shape.

The existing comment said moving to `2524b0be` would be "a separate,
separately-gated change". **This is that change, and it is gated:**
`variance.rs`'s `u8xN::abs_diff` / `i16xN::madd_adjacent` calls — the reason
#96 was pinned in the first place — were rebuilt and re-tested against the
bumped rev (`cargo test -p zenav1-svt-dsp --lib`, 255/255 including the sse
tier tests), not assumed to survive. The comment is updated to say the block
now carries #95 + #96 + #97 and that deleting it takes all three.

**RISK, unchanged by the bump and restated rather than engineered around: this
pins `main` to an UNMERGED pull-request rev on a branch
(`feat/w512-signed-bitcasts`, based on `feat/integer-pairwise-absdiff`) whose
author can still rewrite it. If either branch is force-pushed or deleted before
the PRs merge, `2524b0be` can be garbage-collected and THIS REPO STOPS BUILDING
FOR EVERYONE.** The fix if it fires is to move the rev — one line — not to
redesign anything. The DELETE-ON-RELEASE instruction stays.

=================== 7. GATES — INCOMPLETE, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS WIP ===================

**This work is NOT on `main`. It is a `wip:` commit in the `v4x2` workspace.**
The reason is here and nowhere else: the gate chain did not finish. Nothing was
relaxed, nothing was skipped to make it look landable, and no gate that ran
failed.

WHAT RAN, on the REBASED tree (`main@origin d03f9735` + this change), aarch64:

  cargo nextest run --workspace     2547 / 2547
  tier witness                      ["NeonToken", "ScalarToken"]
  identity_full_8bit.sh             1100 / 1100
  six pinned still cells            290/839/63/171/580/693 B, all OK
  regression_spotcheck.sh           104 / 104
  inter_byte_gate.sh                PASS, 108 required, 0 failed, 1 known-open
  inter_completion_scan SCAN_GATE=1 was still running when the session ended

WHAT DID NOT RUN, and must before this lands:

  * **x86-64 (r7900x): the ENTIRE gate chain.** An earlier chain was started
    and then deliberately killed when the tree was rebased onto `d03f9735`
    (the `sse`/`percall` lane's tree, which changes `variance.rs` — encoder
    source, not just benchmarks), because gates on a superseded tree are worth
    nothing. It was not restarted before the session ended. **The x86 column is
    EMPTY. On x86 this change also selects the `_v4` arm, which is exactly the
    arm no aarch64 gate can exercise, so this is the important half.**
  * aarch64: `video_key_matrix.sh`, `screen_ibc_byte_gate.sh`,
    `screen_palette_gate.sh`, and the tail of `inter_completion_scan.sh`.
  * Both: `fctx_gate.sh`, `decode_conformance.sh`, `decode_gate_grid.sh`.
    (`decode_gate_grid` is no longer a coverage gap — dav1d is installed on
    both Linux boxes now — so it belongs in the chain and was never reached.)

The dsp crate's own suite DID run against the bumped pin on both ISAs
(`cargo test -p zenav1-svt-dsp --lib`, 255 / 255), which is what verifies that
`variance.rs`'s `u8xN::abs_diff` / `i16xN::madd_adjacent` still compile and
still produce the same answers at `2524b0be` — the specific thing the pin bump
risked.
